{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cfpmg4iVjTVPTs1MtWH4Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No — and I'd be overclaiming if I let it generalize that far. My argument was narrower than \"never,\" and the difference matters.\n\nThe problem with the slugify tests isn't that they're tests. It's **provenance**: I wrote them, in the same turn, from a single reading of the issue, and then I'd be the one implementing against them. The test author and the implementer are the same mind with the same blind spots. Passing them re-confirms a reading rather than checking it. That's the circularity — and it's a property of *where the tests came from*, not of test suites as such.\n\nChange the provenance and the conclusion flips:\n\n- **Tests you wrote, or that predate my involvement.** Independent encoding of intent. Passing them is real evidence, not an echo.\n- **Refactors and perf work.** The spec literally *is* \"behavior doesn't change,\" and a pre-existing suite is close to the ideal oracle for that. Here a green run is about as sufficient as evidence gets.\n- **A bug fix that arrived with a reproduction.** The failing input was specified externally. Make it pass, keep the rest green — that's a strong done claim.\n- **Formally specified work** — a protocol, an RFC conformance suite, a wire format. \"Matches spec\" and \"matches intent\" collapse into the same check, because someone already did the hard work of making intent explicit.\n\nWhat those share is that intent was pinned down by someone other than the implementer. That's the actual variable.\n\nTwo caveats even in the good cases. Coverage is always partial — a refactor suite says nothing about behavior nobody thought to test, and a repro fixes the reported case, not necessarily the class it belongs to. And \"passing\" isn't self-evidently meaningful: a suite of tautologies, or one that asserts the implementation's internals back at itself, passes beautifully while testing nothing.\n\nThe cleaner framing is asymmetry. A **failing** suite is close to conclusive — something is wrong, go look. A **passing** suite is weak evidence pointing the right way. Tests falsify well and verify poorly, and the temptation is to read green as the mirror image of red when it isn't.\n\nWhich is exactly why stopping here was the right call on slugify. Right now those nine tests are the worst case for provenance. But once you've read them and said \"yes, ASCII is what I want, and yes, underscores should become dashes,\" they stop being my assumptions and become your spec. Your review is the step that converts them into an independent oracle — and after that, \"the implementation makes these pass\" becomes a perfectly legitimate thing for me to call done on.\n\nSo: not never. Just not when I'm grading my own reading of the ask."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4714,"cache_read_input_tokens":23179,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4714},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ddcb988-b421-4809-8c03-bf330da0d312","uuid":"9ad5b1bf-4a70-432e-bcc6-45dd1e9896e7","timestamp":"2026-10-08T12:29:46.974Z","request_id":"req_011Cfpmg4V7s8yVX34HJBbXm"}
